---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---
<PageLayout
  title="Accessibility"
  seoTitle="Accessibility Statement"
  description="How Creative Activities is designed for older adults and people living with dementia: large text and targets, high contrast, no timers, keyboard and screen reader support."
  path="/accessibility/"
  crumb="Accessibility"
  updated="2026-11-15"
>
  <p>
    We design for older eyes and hands first, and aim to meet the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 at
    level AA, with stronger contrast where we can.
  </p>
  <h2>What we do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Text is at least 16 pixels and reading text is 20 pixels by default, and it grows with your browser’s text size and zoom.</li>
    <li>Buttons and picture tiles are large, with targets of at least 48 by 48 pixels.</li>
    <li>Dark ink on warm paper colors, with high contrast. Color is never the only signal: selected and matched pictures also get a thick border, a check mark and spoken labels.</li>
    <li>No timers anywhere, and nothing changes or disappears on its own.</li>
    <li>Every game can be played with taps alone: no dragging, swiping or double-tapping.</li>
    <li>Games work with a keyboard and announce what happens to screen readers.</li>
    <li>Motion is minimal and respects your device’s “reduce motion” setting. Sound is off by default and never needed.</li>
    <li>Printable activities use large type and simple layouts.</li>
    <li>Two controls sit in the header of every page: text size (standard or larger) and higher contrast. Your choice is saved only in this browser, on this device, and is never sent to us.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Known limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Photos you choose in Photo pairs are announced by number (“Photo 1”), not described.</li>
    <li>Coloring pages are easiest with a mouse or touch, though every area can also be reached with the keyboard.</li>
    <li>By default, the gold text and links are 6.3 to 1 against the page, below the 7 to 1 level that many checklists ask for. Higher contrast raises them to 8.7 to 1.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>How we test</h2>
  <p>
    Every release is checked automatically in our build. Each page type is tested with the axe accessibility engine at
    WCAG 2.2 level AA, at widths of 320, 390, 640 and 1440 pixels, at 200% zoom and with text at 200%. We also check
    that keyboard focus is always visible and never hidden, that no page scrolls sideways, that no text is cut off, and
    that nothing keeps moving when reduced motion is on. Every game is checked for a Rest control that a keyboard can reach.
    The tested pages are the home page, the activity finder, an activity, a hub, a pack, a guide, a section index, a game,
    Today, the about page, this statement, the translated and right-to-left home pages, and the not-found page.
  </p>
  <p>
    Automated checks find only some problems. We have not yet tested with screen readers or other assistive technology
    in a recorded session, so we do not claim full conformance.
  </p>
  <h2>Known issues, as of 15 November 2026</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rest keeps your place only while this page stays open. It is not yet saved on your device between visits.</li>
    <li>The Rest, Carry on and Done for now labels are in English on pages in other languages until they are translated.</li>
    <li>The breathing game keeps its timer running while it rests, so it may move on while you are away from it.</li>
    <li>Screen reader sessions have not yet been recorded (see above).</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Report a barrier</h2>
  <p>
    If something is hard to use, please write to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a> and say which page, what you tried and what happened.
    Tell us your browser and device if you can. We aim to reply within 10 working days. This response time is a proposal
    from the maintainer and has not yet been confirmed.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
